n year, led by utility and data-centre customers.\n- Cancellations were below 1%.\n- Backlog is not the same as 2027 revenue. Some of it may convert in 2026 or after 2027, and that timing is not disclosed.\n\n**Run-rate** (n2.d3)\n- Quarterly revenue in C$M was 905 (2025Q3), 960 (2025Q4), 980 (2026Q1) and 890 (2026Q2).\n- Q2 was depressed by the Halbrook fire.\n- Before the fire the run-rate was roughly C$3.85–3.9B a year (980 × 4 ≈ 3.9B). Hitting C$4.2B requires about 8% growth over that.\n- Q1 2026 was up about 8% on a year earlier only if 2025Q1 was near 905, which I haven't seen.\n- Orders growing 11% make that growth plausible.\n\n**Operations** (n2.d5)\n- Halbrook is Voltaris's largest plant. It was offline for nine weeks in Q2 and restarted in August at full capacity.\n- Management expects to recover the lost shipments by mid-2027.\n- That means some deferred revenue lands in 2027, which supports the guidance. It also means the plant must run above normal for a while, which is an execution risk.\n- The Q2 shortfall was about C$90M, taking Q2 against a Q1-like run-rate of about C$980M. That is small next to the C$900M of headroom in backlog above guidance.\n\n**Conclusion**\n- The base case is about C$4.1–4.3B, centred near the guidance.\n- Upside comes from catch-up shipments and 11% order growth.\n- Downside comes from further plant disruption, slower backlog conversion, or a ramp that doesn't reach the growth rate needed.\n- I found no H2 2026 results, so the recovery is unverified.","ttft_ms":1774,"type":"result","duration_ms":33421,"uuid":"a2967cfe-bdb6-4377-80dd-6524e589c686","ttft_stream_ms":884,"time_to_request_ms":66,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":4,"system_prompt":7,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":5,"input_attachments":10,"input_hooks":2,"conversation_append":0,"transcript_persist":2,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":5,"init_frame_send":6,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":3,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":0,"autocompact":1,"request_a